WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.530 --> 00:00:05.500
Despite the declining success of the Batman
film franchise in the '90s, Warner Bros. knew

00:00:05.500 --> 00:00:08.760
that in the right hands, the character was
still a goldmine.

00:00:08.809 --> 00:00:13.420
Even though two-time Batman director Joel
Schumacher was interested in making another

00:00:13.420 --> 00:00:15.839
installment, the studio wanted to start fresh.

00:00:15.980 --> 00:00:20.700
That's how Darren Aronofsky got a deal to
pen a screenplay with Frank Miller to adapt

00:00:20.700 --> 00:00:23.060
Miller's comic Batman: Year One.

00:00:23.160 --> 00:00:27.560
That story arc was eventually made as an animated
film, but Aronofsky and Miller's screenplay

00:00:27.560 --> 00:00:28.680
never got developed.

00:00:28.940 --> 00:00:30.900
Here's why we never got to see it.

00:00:31.740 --> 00:00:32.840
A darker knight

00:00:33.660 --> 00:00:39.020
The Year One comic follows Bruce Wayne's return
to Gotham after 12 years of travel and training.

00:00:39.190 --> 00:00:43.740
He befriends a young Jim Gordon and the two
take down gangsters within Carmine Falcone's

00:00:43.740 --> 00:00:44.820
mob organization.

00:00:45.060 --> 00:00:49.909
Despite citing Year One as their inspiration,
Aronofsky and Miller changed a lot of aspects

00:00:49.909 --> 00:00:54.499
of the comic for the screenplay, such as making
young Bruce Wayne live as a bum while learning

00:00:54.500 --> 00:00:55.900
how to fight crime from books.

00:00:56.080 --> 00:01:00.260
The big takeaway, according to Miller, was
that the script just strayed too far from

00:01:00.260 --> 00:01:05.160
traditional Batman storylines, and included
elements that took the film in some dark directions.

00:01:05.360 --> 00:01:11.060
Considering PG-13 is as far as Warner Bros.
has ever gone with Batman, this new take on

00:01:11.060 --> 00:01:13.520
Batman was just too far from family friendly.

00:01:14.180 --> 00:01:15.460
Bait and switch

00:01:16.100 --> 00:01:20.180
Darren Aronofsky later admitted that he was
never particularly interested in making a

00:01:20.180 --> 00:01:20.880
Batman movie.

00:01:21.000 --> 00:01:24.760
He was trying to make a different movie, and
he saw Year One as a way to get the studio

00:01:24.760 --> 00:01:26.280
behind his indie effort.

00:01:26.440 --> 00:01:27.200
He explained:

00:01:27.440 --> 00:01:29.800
“It was a kind of bait and switch strategy.

00:01:29.800 --> 00:01:33.500
I was working on Requiem for a Dream, and
I got a phone call that Warner Bros. wanted

00:01:33.500 --> 00:01:34.760
to talk about Batman.

00:01:35.000 --> 00:01:39.000
At the time I had this idea for a film called
The Fountain, which I knew was gonna be this

00:01:39.000 --> 00:01:43.180
big movie [...] My theory was if I can write
this Batman film and they could perceive me

00:01:43.180 --> 00:01:45.520
as a writer for it, then maybe they'd let
me go ahead."

00:01:45.680 --> 00:01:49.929
Even though the Batman movie never materialized,
Warner Bros. did finally give him funding

00:01:49.929 --> 00:01:54.360
to make The Fountain in 2006, so at least
some good came out of the whole thing.

00:01:55.020 --> 00:01:55.960
Tough cast

00:01:56.540 --> 00:02:01.119
According to the book Tales From Development
Hell, Aronofsky's original pitch is what intrigued

00:02:01.120 --> 00:02:03.980
Warner Bros. enough to give him a crack at
The Caped Crusader.

00:02:04.160 --> 00:02:08.479
Aronofsky's idea was to take the movie in
a gritty, realistic direction, and his main

00:02:08.480 --> 00:02:10.340
choice for the dark, gritty hero?

00:02:10.760 --> 00:02:11.759
Clint Eastwood.

00:02:11.940 --> 00:02:15.240
"You've got to ask yourself one question,
"Do I feel lucky?"

00:02:16.320 --> 00:02:18.080
Well, do ya, punk?"

00:02:18.640 --> 00:02:23.100
Dirty Harry in a Batsuit sounds like a great
idea, but that casting decision didn't pan

00:02:23.100 --> 00:02:23.600
out.

00:02:23.800 --> 00:02:29.380
Aronofsky then settled on Year One as his
source material, and according to a 2017 Yahoo!

00:02:29.380 --> 00:02:33.140
interview, he wanted to cast Joaquin Phoenix
as the younger Bruce Wayne.

00:02:33.240 --> 00:02:37.360
Neither of his casting choices seemed to give
Warner Bros. much confidence, and Aronofsky

00:02:37.360 --> 00:02:41.700
later suggested that the entire concept might
have just been too far ahead of its time.

00:02:42.200 --> 00:02:43.340
Inexperienced

00:02:43.860 --> 00:02:49.040
With only Robocop 2 and Robocop 3 as screenwriting
credits at this point, Frank Miller admitted

00:02:49.049 --> 00:02:51.239
he had a lot to learn about the movie business.

00:02:51.240 --> 00:02:51.920
He explained:

00:02:52.040 --> 00:02:54.360
"The whole process was one of discovery for
me.

00:02:54.370 --> 00:02:56.060
I had to figure out what they wanted.

00:02:56.060 --> 00:02:59.269
Normally somebody like me, if you say you
want to make a movie, I’ll come up with

00:02:59.269 --> 00:03:02.820
something that will be 12 hours long and cost
$1 billion to make."

00:03:03.460 --> 00:03:04.680
Premature prequel

00:03:05.200 --> 00:03:09.470
Even without the gritty, R-rated tone the
two filmmakers wanted for Year One, there's

00:03:09.470 --> 00:03:12.280
a chance the movie would have eventually been
canned anyway.

00:03:12.480 --> 00:03:18.060
Prequels and spin-offs may be a big market
now, but in 1999, most movie studios preferred

00:03:18.060 --> 00:03:20.520
to bank on sequels to keep a franchise going.

00:03:20.620 --> 00:03:25.480
So when Aronofsky and Miller decided to wipe
out the entire Batman film timeline to start

00:03:25.480 --> 00:03:28.560
fresh with Year One, the studio may have been
hesitant.

00:03:28.680 --> 00:03:32.900
Ultimately, the possibility of new toys and
merchandise might have been the deciding factor

00:03:32.900 --> 00:03:36.549
in Warner Bros. agreeing to start working
on the film, although that angle didn't pan

00:03:36.549 --> 00:03:37.280
out either.

00:03:38.040 --> 00:03:39.120
Toy bust

00:03:39.620 --> 00:03:44.780
After giving Warner Bros. his and Aronofsky's
script, Miller quickly realized that Batman:

00:03:44.799 --> 00:03:47.000
Year One probably wasn't going to pan out.

00:03:47.000 --> 00:03:48.900
Describing how the studio reacted, he said:

00:03:48.900 --> 00:03:51.440
"I think I heard a shriek of horror at first.

00:03:51.440 --> 00:03:53.380
They were shocked at how bold it was."

00:03:53.720 --> 00:03:56.920
In other words, it wasn't going to be a movie
that sold a lot of toys.

00:03:56.980 --> 00:04:00.900
Miller spelled it out clearly when he recalled
being told that the project wasn't moving

00:04:00.910 --> 00:04:04.689
forward: "The executive wanted to do a Batman
he could take his kids to.

00:04:04.689 --> 00:04:05.860
And this wasn't that.

00:04:05.860 --> 00:04:07.300
It didn't have the toys in it."

00:04:07.540 --> 00:04:08.810
"Who defends this city?

00:04:08.810 --> 00:04:09.740
Batman and Robin."

00:04:10.200 --> 00:04:13.950
And that's ultimately what sunk Year One:
Warner Bros. didn't feel like it was the right

00:04:13.950 --> 00:04:15.750
fit for a Burger King tie in.

00:04:15.750 --> 00:04:20.200
That's too bad, because it definitely sounds
like the best Batman film we never saw.

00:04:20.700 --> 00:04:21.720
Thanks for watching!

